      "side": 1,
      "message": "@roybabayov@gmail.com thanks for putting that patch up.  I know for me it was a surprise when I found lex doing this.\n\nAs @martin@meltin.net, pointed out the higher CIDR layer is checking the CIDR.  The lexer just pulls out the tokens.\n\nFor the patch in  https://review.gerrithub.io/c/ffilz/nfs-ganesha/+/1219764, I did look at making the mask more exact but having a look at it I realised it was more detrimental.  You stop the CIDR parsing from giving a better error message.  \n\nYesterday I tried it out with the expressions from this patch and the other patch.\n\nWith the simpler regular expression, we get errors such as\n\n2025-08-26T07:39:00.178016+0000 : epoch 00000005 : nsds1 : ganesha.nfsd-9744[main] config_errs_to_log :CONFIG :CRIT :Config File (/etc/ganesha/nsds_ganesha/exports.conf:29): Expected a IPv4 CIDR address, got (192.168.10.0/94)\n\nWith the more complex regular expression you get errors such as\n2025-08-26T08:06:40.186544+0000 : epoch 00000005 : nsds1 : ganesha.nfsd-5867[main] config_errs_to_log :CONFIG :CRIT :Config \nFile (/etc/ganesha/nsds_ganesha/exports.conf:29): Expected a client, got a file path name for (192.168.10.0/41)\n\nSo with the more complex rgex it isn\u0027t recognised as a CIDR by the lexer - just the next expression that matches.",
